<!-- livebook:{"persist_outputs":true} -->

# Bounded Authority Report Adapter: every signer in one notebook

## What this notebook shows

This notebook runs every signing entry point the adapter exposes, verifies each result with the
public `bounded_authority_protocol` package, and then shows each check failing closed. It needs no
database, no container and no other running service. Every key is generated in the notebook when
it runs. Run the cells in order; the value under each cell is the result it returns.

It runs against this repository's source: version 0.10.0 in `mix.exs`, which declares
`bounded_authority_protocol` `~> 0.8.0`. That range is unreleased; the published 0.10.0 release
pins `== 0.8.0` (see the CHANGELOG). The repository's test suite runs these same cells against
the repository source and compares each result with the value pinned under its cell, so a cell
that stops running, or starts returning something else, fails the build.

## The problem this solves

An API key answers "who sent this?" It does not answer "were they allowed to do this?" If an API
key leaks, whoever has it can send anything until it is rotated.

The Bounded Authority Protocol answers the second question. A request carries a **grant**, signed
ahead of time by an issuer, that names one holder key and the operations that key may perform. The
holder adds a **proof**: a signature made with its own key, at the moment of the request, over that
grant and that exact request. A leaked grant is useless without the holder's key, and a proof made
for one request does not verify for another.

## Three roles, kept separate on purpose

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant Issuer as Issuer<br/>(the authority runtime)
    participant Holder as Holder<br/>(the edge, using THIS adapter)
    participant Verifier as Verifier<br/>(any party)
    Note over Issuer: holds the issuer key
    Note over Holder: holds the holder key
    Note over Verifier: holds only PUBLIC keys
    Issuer->>Holder: a signed grant (ahead of time)
    Holder->>Holder: sign_report(report, holder_handle)<br/>signs a PROOF over grant + request
    Holder->>Verifier: the request + {grant, proof}
    Verifier->>Verifier: check_envelope(credentials, expected)<br/>against published public keys
    Verifier-->>Holder: accept or reject, offline
```

* **The verifier never holds a signing key**, so a compromised verifier cannot forge anything.
* **The issuer never sees each request.** The holder proves possession offline, with no call home.

The shape is the same as DPoP (RFC 9449). The adapter's one job is the **holder** box: it signs.
It never verifies, transports or stores anything. The same signing path also serves the other
objects a key holder signs (an issuer's grant, a chain checkpoint, a key rotation, a content
assertion), so this notebook covers each of them too.

| Entry point                    | Signs                                          | Suite                  | Verified with                            |
| ------------------------------ | ---------------------------------------------- | ---------------------- | ---------------------------------------- |
| `sign_report/3`                | holder proof                                   | Ed25519 (major 1)      | `V1.check_envelope/2`                    |
| `sign_local_loopback_report/3` | holder proof for a loopback listener           | Ed25519 (major 1)      | `LocalLoopbackHttp.V1.check_envelope/2`  |
| `sign_grant/3`                 | grant (issuer role, optional role attestation) | Ed25519 (major 1)      | `V1.verify_grant/3`                      |
| `sign_anchor/3`                | boundary anchor (chain checkpoint)             | Ed25519 (major 1)      | `V1.verify_historical_anchor/3`          |
| `sign_key_transition/3`        | key transition (rotation)                      | Ed25519 (major 1)      | `V1.verify_key_transition/4`             |
| `sign_content_assertion/3`     | content assertion                              | Ed25519 (own profile)  | `ContentAssertion.V1.verify_assertion/2` |
| `V3.sign_report/3`             | holder proof                                   | ES256, P-256 (major 3) | `V3.check_envelope/2`                    |
| `V3.sign_grant/3`              | grant (issuer role)                            | ES256, P-256 (major 3) | `V3.verify_grant/3`                      |
| `V3.sign_anchor/3`             | boundary anchor                                | ES256, P-256 (major 3) | `V3.verify_historical_anchor/3`          |
| `V3.sign_key_transition/3`     | key transition                                 | ES256, P-256 (major 3) | `V3.verify_key_transition/4`             |

The adapter has no role-attestation signer: a role attestation is signed by the authority. The
adapter consumes one, through `sign_grant/3`'s `:role_attestation` option, as a gate before it
signs a grant.

## Setup

The adapter comes from this repository; the protocol package resolves from Hex.

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:bounded_authority_report_adapter, path: Path.expand("..", __DIR__)}
])
```

```elixir
alias BoundedAuthorityProtocol.V1
alias BoundedAuthorityProtocol.V3
alias BoundedAuthorityProtocol.ApplicationProfile.LocalLoopbackHttp.V1, as: LocalLoopback
alias BoundedAuthorityProtocol.ContentAssertion.V1, as: ContentAssertion
alias BoundedAuthorityProtocol.RoleAttestation.V1, as: RoleAttestation
alias BoundedAuthorityReportAdapter, as: Adapter

to_string(Application.spec(:bounded_authority_report_adapter, :vsn))
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
"0.10.0"
```

## Key handles: the adapter signs without ever holding a private key

The adapter never receives a private key. The caller passes a `{module, term}` **handle**: a
module that implements the `BoundedAuthorityReportAdapter` behavior, and an opaque term only that
module understands. In production the module forwards the message to an HSM, a cloud KMS or a key
server. Here the term is a map with the key pair in it, which is acceptable only in a demo.

Two handles cover both suites. The P-256 handle converts OTP's DER signature to the raw `r || s`
form ES256 requires, and then deliberately returns the **high-S** spelling of the signature. Many
signing devices return either spelling; this one always returns the one the protocol refuses, so
the ES256 section below can show the adapter normalizing it.

```elixir
defmodule Demo.Ed25519Handle do
  @moduledoc false
  @behaviour BoundedAuthorityReportAdapter

  @impl true
  def sign(message, %{private: private}),
    do: {:ok, :crypto.sign(:eddsa, :none, message, [private, :ed25519])}

  @impl true
  def public_key(%{public: public}), do: {:ok, public}

  # The raw RFC 7638 thumbprint, from the protocol package's documented JWK functions.
  @impl true
  def thumbprint(%{public: public}) do
    with {:ok, jwk} <- BoundedAuthorityProtocol.V1.Jwk.encode_public(public, %{}),
         {:ok, thumbprint} <- BoundedAuthorityProtocol.V1.Jwk.thumbprint(jwk, %{}) do
      {:ok, Base.url_decode64!(thumbprint, padding: false)}
    end
  end

  # Key id and public key in one call, so a rotating key store cannot split them.
  @impl true
  def key_identity(%{key_id: key_id, public: public}), do: {:ok, {key_id, public}}

  # Role, key id and public key in one call; sign_grant/3 requires the :issuer role.
  @impl true
  def signing_identity(%{role: role, key_id: key_id, public: public}),
    do: {:ok, {role, key_id, public}}
end

defmodule Demo.P256Handle do
  @moduledoc false
  @behaviour BoundedAuthorityReportAdapter

  # The order of the P-256 group.
  @n 0xFFFFFFFF00000000FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFBCE6FAADA7179E84F3B9CAC2FC632551

  @impl true
  def sign(message, %{private: private}) do
    der = :crypto.sign(:ecdsa, :sha256, message, [private, :prime256v1])
    <<0x30, _, 0x02, r_size, r::binary-size(r_size), 0x02, s_size, s::binary-size(s_size)>> = der
    r = :binary.decode_unsigned(r)
    s = :binary.decode_unsigned(s)
    high_s = if s > div(@n, 2), do: s, else: @n - s
    {:ok, <<r::unsigned-big-256, high_s::unsigned-big-256>>}
  end

  @impl true
  def public_key(%{public: public}), do: {:ok, public}

  @impl true
  def thumbprint(%{public: public}) do
    with {:ok, jwk} <- BoundedAuthorityProtocol.V3.EcJwk.encode_public(public, %{}),
         {:ok, thumbprint} <- BoundedAuthorityProtocol.V3.EcJwk.thumbprint(jwk, %{}) do
      {:ok, Base.url_decode64!(thumbprint, padding: false)}
    end
  end

  @impl true
  def key_identity(%{key_id: key_id, public: public}), do: {:ok, {key_id, public}}

  @impl true
  def signing_identity(%{role: role, key_id: key_id, public: public}),
    do: {:ok, {role, key_id, public}}
end

[Demo.Ed25519Handle, Demo.P256Handle]
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
[Demo.Ed25519Handle, Demo.P256Handle]
```

## Keys and small helpers

Each party would keep its own key in its own place. They share one notebook here only so the whole
loop can run in one process.

```elixir
now = System.system_time(:second)

ed25519_key = fn key_id, role ->
  {public, private} = :crypto.generate_key(:eddsa, :ed25519)
  %{key_id: key_id, role: role, public: public, private: private}
end

p256_key = fn key_id, role ->
  {public, private} = :crypto.generate_key(:ecdh, :prime256v1)
  %{key_id: key_id, role: role, public: public, private: private}
end

# Ed25519 keys (contract-major 1 and the content-assertion profile).
issuer = ed25519_key.("issuer-key-1", :issuer)
holder = ed25519_key.("holder-key-1", :holder)
attestor = ed25519_key.("attestor-key-1", :attestor)
chain_key = ed25519_key.("chain-key-1", :chain)
next_chain_key = ed25519_key.("chain-key-2", :chain)
publisher = ed25519_key.("publisher-key-1", :publisher)

# P-256 keys (contract-major 3, ES256).
ec_issuer = p256_key.("issuer-ec-key-1", :issuer)
ec_holder = p256_key.("holder-ec-key-1", :holder)
ec_chain_key = p256_key.("chain-ec-key-1", :chain)
ec_next_chain_key = p256_key.("chain-ec-key-2", :chain)

# What a verifier is told about a key: its id, its public half, and when it is valid.
validity = fn key ->
  %V1.HistoricalPublicKey{
    key_id: key.key_id,
    public_key: key.public,
    valid_from: now - 3_600,
    valid_before: now + 3_600
  }
end

# Selected fields of a result, in a fixed order (a map would print in no fixed order).
facts_of = fn struct, keys -> Enum.map(keys, &{&1, Map.fetch!(struct, &1)}) end

# Flip one bit in the first byte of a compact's signature segment.
flip_one_byte = fn compact ->
  [header, payload, signature] = String.split(compact, ".")
  <<first, rest::binary>> = Base.url_decode64!(signature, padding: false)
  flipped = Base.url_encode64(<<Bitwise.bxor(first, 1), rest::binary>>, padding: false)
  Enum.join([header, payload, flipped], ".")
end

[
  ed25519_public_key_bytes: byte_size(holder.public),
  p256_public_key_bytes: byte_size(ec_holder.public)
]
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
[ed25519_public_key_bytes: 32, p256_public_key_bytes: 65]
```

## Part 1: Ed25519 (contract-major 1)

### Role 1, issuer: sign a grant

In production the authority runtime issues grants. The adapter can sign one for an issuer that
keeps its key behind a handle: `sign_grant/3` takes the key id from the handle, never from the
input, and refuses unless the handle declares the `:issuer` role. The grant below lets one holder
key submit sensor readings and run a tool on a local listener.

```elixir
issuer_handle = {Demo.Ed25519Handle, issuer}
holder_handle = {Demo.Ed25519Handle, holder}
{:ok, holder_thumbprint} = Demo.Ed25519Handle.thumbprint(holder)

grant_input = %{
  issuer: "https://issuer.example",
  grant_id: "urn:example:grant:greenhouse-1",
  audiences: ["https://telemetry.example"],
  issued_at: now - 60,
  not_before: now - 60,
  expires_at: now + 3_600,
  holder_thumbprint: holder_thumbprint,
  operations: [
    %V1.Operation{name: "submit_reading", selectors: [:all]},
    %V1.Operation{name: "run_local_tool", selectors: [:all]}
  ]
}

{:ok, %{grant: grant}} = Adapter.sign_grant(grant_input, issuer_handle)

trusted_issuer = %V1.TrustedIssuer{key_id: issuer.key_id, public_key: issuer.public}

expected_grant = %V1.ExpectedGrant{
  issuer: "https://issuer.example",
  audience: "https://telemetry.example",
  evaluation_time: now,
  clock_skew: 60,
  bounds: V1.Bounds.maximum()
}

{:ok, grant_facts} = V1.verify_grant(grant, trusted_issuer, expected_grant)
facts_of.(grant_facts, [:version, :grant_id, :matched_audience, :authorization])
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
[
  version: 1,
  grant_id: "urn:example:grant:greenhouse-1",
  matched_audience: "https://telemetry.example",
  authorization: :not_evaluated
]
```

`authorization: :not_evaluated` is deliberate: a verified grant proves only that the bytes are
genuine. Whether to act on it is the consumer's policy decision.

The grant fails closed. A handle that declares the holder role cannot sign one, and the verifier
rejects a changed byte, the wrong issuer key, and an audience the grant does not name.

```elixir
holder_declared_issuer_key = {Demo.Ed25519Handle, %{issuer | role: :holder}}

[
  holder_role_handle: Adapter.sign_grant(grant_input, holder_declared_issuer_key),
  tampered_grant: V1.verify_grant(flip_one_byte.(grant), trusted_issuer, expected_grant),
  wrong_issuer_key:
    V1.verify_grant(grant, %{trusted_issuer | public_key: holder.public}, expected_grant),
  wrong_audience:
    V1.verify_grant(grant, trusted_issuer, %{expected_grant | audience: "https://other.example"})
]
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
[
  holder_role_handle: {:error, :invalid_key_handle},
  tampered_grant: {:error, :invalid},
  wrong_issuer_key: {:error, :invalid},
  wrong_audience: {:error, :invalid}
]
```

### The role-attestation gate on `sign_grant/3`

A handle's role is only its own claim. With `:role_attestation`, `sign_grant/3` first checks an
attestation signed by the authority (`bap-role-attestation/1`) that binds the issuer role to the
handle's own key, and refuses before it signs anything if the check fails. The expired case
uses an attestation that was valid a minute ago: evaluated at a time inside its window it passes,
so only the expiry is being tested. The authority signs
the attestation with the protocol package; this is not the adapter's job.

```elixir
attest = fn attestation ->
  {:ok, input} = RoleAttestation.attestation_signing_input(attestation, %{})
  signature = :crypto.sign(:eddsa, :none, input.message, [attestor.private, :ed25519])
  {:ok, compact} = RoleAttestation.assemble_compact(input, signature)
  compact
end

issuer_attestation = %RoleAttestation.RoleAttestation{
  attestor_key_id: attestor.key_id,
  jti: "urn:example:attestation:1",
  key_id: issuer.key_id,
  public_key: issuer.public,
  role: "issuer",
  nbf: now - 60,
  exp: now + 3_600
}

gate = fn attestation, at ->
  %{role_attestation: %{compact: attest.(attestation), attestor: validity.(attestor), now: at}}
end

# Valid from ten minutes ago until one minute ago; the attestor key itself is valid throughout.
expired_attestation = %{
  issuer_attestation
  | jti: "urn:example:attestation:2",
    nbf: now - 600,
    exp: now - 60
}

attested_grant =
  with {:ok, %{grant: attested}} <-
         Adapter.sign_grant(grant_input, issuer_handle, gate.(issuer_attestation, now)),
       {:ok, _facts} <- V1.verify_grant(attested, trusted_issuer, expected_grant) do
    :signed_and_verified
  end

[
  issuer_attested: attested_grant,
  holder_attested:
    Adapter.sign_grant(
      grant_input,
      issuer_handle,
      gate.(%{issuer_attestation | role: "holder"}, now)
    ),
  attests_another_key:
    Adapter.sign_grant(
      grant_input,
      issuer_handle,
      gate.(%{issuer_attestation | key_id: holder.key_id, public_key: holder.public}, now)
    ),
  attestation_expired:
    Adapter.sign_grant(grant_input, issuer_handle, gate.(expired_attestation, now)),
  same_attestation_before_expiry:
    elem(Adapter.sign_grant(grant_input, issuer_handle, gate.(expired_attestation, now - 120)), 0)
]
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
[
  issuer_attested: :signed_and_verified,
  holder_attested: {:error, :invalid_role_attestation},
  attests_another_key: {:error, :invalid_role_attestation},
  attestation_expired: {:error, :invalid_role_attestation},
  same_attestation_before_expiry: :ok
]
```

### Role 2, holder: sign the proof (the adapter's one job)

The edge calls `sign_report/3` with the grant, the request fields and the holder handle. The
adapter builds the proof, signs it through the handle, checks the signature against the holder's
public key, and returns `{grant, proof}`. The grant passes through unchanged.

```elixir
reading = {:object, [{"sensor", {:string, "greenhouse-4"}}, {"celsius", {:integer, 21}}]}

report = %{
  grant_compact: grant,
  operation: "submit_reading",
  method: "POST",
  target_uri: "https://telemetry.example/readings",
  invocation_id: "6f1c2a3b-4d5e-4f60-8a71-92b3c4d5e6f7",
  # The request body in the protocol's tagged JSON form. A real consumer gets this with
  # V1.Json.decode(raw_body_bytes), on both sides, from the same bytes.
  cast_arguments: reading,
  nonce: "nonce-reading-1"
}

{:ok, %{grant: ^grant, proof: proof}} =
  Adapter.sign_report(report, holder_handle, %{issued_at: now - 5})

# Decoding is not verifying; this only shows what the proof binds.
{:ok, decoded_proof} = V1.decode_proof(proof, %{})

facts_of.(decoded_proof, [:operation, :method, :target_uri, :nonce, :verification]) ++
  [holder_matches_grant: decoded_proof.holder_thumbprint == holder_thumbprint]
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
[
  operation: "submit_reading",
  method: "POST",
  target_uri: "https://telemetry.example/readings",
  nonce: "nonce-reading-1",
  verification: :not_evaluated,
  holder_matches_grant: true
]
```

### Role 3, verifier: check the envelope

The verifier rebuilds what it expects the request to be and checks the grant and proof together
against public keys only, offline.

```elixir
credentials = %V1.Credentials{grant: grant, proof: proof}

expected = %V1.ExpectedRequest{
  trusted_issuer: trusted_issuer,
  issuer: "https://issuer.example",
  audience: "https://telemetry.example",
  method: "POST",
  target_uri: "https://telemetry.example/readings",
  invocation_id: "6f1c2a3b-4d5e-4f60-8a71-92b3c4d5e6f7",
  operation: "submit_reading",
  cast_arguments: reading,
  evaluation_time: now,
  clock_skew: 60,
  proof_max_age: 300,
  nonce: {:required, "nonce-reading-1"},
  bounds: V1.Bounds.maximum()
}

{:ok, envelope_facts} = V1.check_envelope(credentials, expected)
facts_of.(envelope_facts, [:version, :operation, :matched_audience, :authorization])
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
[
  version: 1,
  operation: "submit_reading",
  matched_audience: "https://telemetry.example",
  authorization: :not_evaluated
]
```

### The proof fails closed

A changed byte breaks the signature. A proof from a key other than the one the grant names is
refused. So is the envelope when the audience, the request body or the nonce differs from what the
verifier expects.

```elixir
stranger_handle = {Demo.Ed25519Handle, ed25519_key.("stranger-key-1", :holder)}

{:ok, %{proof: stranger_proof}} =
  Adapter.sign_report(report, stranger_handle, %{issued_at: now - 5})

hotter = {:object, [{"sensor", {:string, "greenhouse-4"}}, {"celsius", {:integer, 35}}]}

[
  tampered_proof: V1.check_envelope(%{credentials | proof: flip_one_byte.(proof)}, expected),
  stranger_holder_key: V1.check_envelope(%{credentials | proof: stranger_proof}, expected),
  wrong_audience: V1.check_envelope(credentials, %{expected | audience: "https://other.example"}),
  different_body: V1.check_envelope(credentials, %{expected | cast_arguments: hotter}),
  different_nonce:
    V1.check_envelope(credentials, %{expected | nonce: {:required, "nonce-reading-2"}})
]
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
[
  tampered_proof: {:error, :invalid},
  stranger_holder_key: {:error, :invalid},
  wrong_audience: {:error, :invalid},
  different_body: {:error, :invalid},
  different_nonce: {:error, :invalid}
]
```

### A listener on the loopback interface: `sign_local_loopback_report/3`

A development listener on `127.0.0.1` or `[::1]` uses a separate proof profile
(`bap-application-proof/local-loopback-http/1`, header `typ: "ba+loopback-proof"`). The function
name selects it; nothing else does. The nonce is required, and the target must be a canonical
literal-loopback `http://` URI. Plain loopback HTTP has no TLS, so the listener's operator must
reserve each nonce to stop replays.

```elixir
local_report = %{
  report
  | operation: "run_local_tool",
    target_uri: "http://127.0.0.1:4000/tools/summarize",
    invocation_id: "0b9d8c7e-6f5a-4b3c-9d2e-1f0a9b8c7d6e",
    cast_arguments: {:object, [{"file", {:string, "notes.txt"}}]},
    nonce: "nonce-local-1"
}

{:ok, %{proof: local_proof}} =
  Adapter.sign_local_loopback_report(local_report, holder_handle, %{issued_at: now - 5})

local_credentials = %V1.Credentials{grant: grant, proof: local_proof}

local_expected = %{
  expected
  | operation: "run_local_tool",
    target_uri: "http://127.0.0.1:4000/tools/summarize",
    invocation_id: "0b9d8c7e-6f5a-4b3c-9d2e-1f0a9b8c7d6e",
    cast_arguments: {:object, [{"file", {:string, "notes.txt"}}]},
    nonce: {:required, "nonce-local-1"}
}

{:ok, local_facts} = LocalLoopback.check_envelope(local_credentials, local_expected)

[
  verified: facts_of.(local_facts, [:operation, :target_uri, :authorization]),
  wrong_nonce:
    LocalLoopback.check_envelope(
      local_credentials,
      %{local_expected | nonce: {:required, "nonce-local-2"}}
    ),
  standard_verifier: V1.check_envelope(local_credentials, local_expected),
  localhost_name:
    Adapter.sign_local_loopback_report(
      %{local_report | target_uri: "http://localhost:4000/tools/summarize"},
      holder_handle
    ),
  missing_nonce: Adapter.sign_local_loopback_report(%{local_report | nonce: nil}, holder_handle)
]
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
[
  verified: [
    operation: "run_local_tool",
    target_uri: "http://127.0.0.1:4000/tools/summarize",
    authorization: :not_evaluated
  ],
  wrong_nonce: {:error, :invalid},
  standard_verifier: {:error, :invalid},
  localhost_name: {:error, {:producer_error, :invalid}},
  missing_nonce: {:error, :invalid_report}
]
```

### A chain checkpoint and a key rotation: `sign_anchor/3` and `sign_key_transition/3`

A boundary anchor is a signed checkpoint of an append-only chain, such as an audit log: its id,
its sequence number and the hash of the chain so far. A key transition is the retiring key's
signed statement naming its successor. For both, the key id comes from the handle's single
`key_identity/1` call, never from the input.

```elixir
chain_handle = {Demo.Ed25519Handle, chain_key}
{:ok, chain_fingerprint} = Demo.Ed25519Handle.thumbprint(chain_key)
{:ok, next_chain_fingerprint} = Demo.Ed25519Handle.thumbprint(next_chain_key)

anchor_input = %{
  anchor_id: "urn:example:anchor:audit-log-42",
  chain_id: "urn:example:chain:audit-log",
  sequence: 42,
  chain_hash: :crypto.hash(:sha256, "audit log entries 1 through 42")
}

{:ok, %{anchor: anchor}} = Adapter.sign_anchor(anchor_input, chain_handle, %{anchored_at: now})

expected_anchor = %V1.ExpectedAnchor{
  anchor_id: "urn:example:anchor:audit-log-42",
  anchored_at: now,
  chain_id: "urn:example:chain:audit-log",
  sequence: 42,
  chain_hash: anchor_input.chain_hash,
  key_id: chain_key.key_id,
  key_fingerprint: chain_fingerprint,
  bounds: V1.Bounds.maximum()
}

transition_input = %{
  transition_id: "urn:example:transition:audit-log-1",
  chain_id: "urn:example:chain:audit-log",
  effective_at: now,
  next_key_id: next_chain_key.key_id,
  next_public_key: next_chain_key.public
}

{:ok, %{key_transition: transition}} = Adapter.sign_key_transition(transition_input, chain_handle)

expected_transition = %V1.ExpectedKeyTransition{
  transition_id: "urn:example:transition:audit-log-1",
  chain_id: "urn:example:chain:audit-log",
  effective_at: now,
  current_key_id: chain_key.key_id,
  current_key_fingerprint: chain_fingerprint,
  next_key_id: next_chain_key.key_id,
  next_key_fingerprint: next_chain_fingerprint,
  bounds: V1.Bounds.maximum()
}

{:ok, anchor_facts} = V1.verify_historical_anchor(anchor, validity.(chain_key), expected_anchor)

{:ok, transition_facts} =
  V1.verify_key_transition(
    transition,
    validity.(chain_key),
    validity.(next_chain_key),
    expected_transition
  )

later_hash = :crypto.hash(:sha256, "audit log entries 1 through 43")

[
  anchor_verified: facts_of.(anchor_facts, [:sequence, :verification, :trust]),
  tampered_anchor:
    V1.verify_historical_anchor(flip_one_byte.(anchor), validity.(chain_key), expected_anchor),
  different_chain_hash:
    V1.verify_historical_anchor(
      anchor,
      validity.(chain_key),
      %{expected_anchor | chain_hash: later_hash}
    ),
  transition_verified: facts_of.(transition_facts, [:verification, :trust]),
  wrong_successor:
    V1.verify_key_transition(
      transition,
      validity.(chain_key),
      %{validity.(next_chain_key) | public_key: holder.public},
      expected_transition
    ),
  rotation_to_itself:
    Adapter.sign_key_transition(
      %{transition_input | next_key_id: chain_key.key_id, next_public_key: chain_key.public},
      chain_handle
    )
]
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
[
  anchor_verified: [sequence: 42, verification: :signature_and_window, trust: :not_evaluated],
  tampered_anchor: {:error, :invalid},
  different_chain_hash: {:error, :invalid},
  transition_verified: [verification: :authenticated_transition, trust: :not_evaluated],
  wrong_successor: {:error, :invalid},
  rotation_to_itself: {:error, {:producer_error, :invalid}}
]
```

### A content assertion: `sign_content_assertion/3`

A content assertion (`bap-content-assertion/1`) binds the SHA-256 digest of exact bytes, here a
published air-quality dataset, to an issuer, an audience, a lineage subject, a schema profile, a
validity window and a predecessor (`gen` and `prev`; the first assertion in a lineage has
`gen: 1` and an all-zero `prev`). It grants nothing; it says only that this key signed these
bytes under these terms.

```elixir
dataset = ~s({"station":"riverside","pm25":[8.1,7.9,8.4]})
{:ok, content_digest} = ContentAssertion.content_digest(dataset, %{})
profile_digest = :crypto.hash(:sha256, "urn:example:profile:air-quality/1 schema")
publisher_handle = {Demo.Ed25519Handle, publisher}

assertion_input = %{
  jti: "urn:example:assertion:riverside-1",
  iss: "urn:example:publisher",
  aud: "urn:example:archive",
  sub: "urn:example:dataset:riverside-air-quality",
  profile: "urn:example:profile:air-quality/1",
  profile_digest: profile_digest,
  content_digest: content_digest,
  gen: 1,
  prev: <<0::256>>,
  iat: now - 60,
  nbf: now - 60,
  exp: now + 3_600
}

{:ok, %{content_assertion: assertion}} =
  Adapter.sign_content_assertion(assertion_input, publisher_handle)

expected_assertion = %ContentAssertion.ExpectedContentAssertion{
  attestor: validity.(publisher),
  issuer: "urn:example:publisher",
  audience: "urn:example:archive",
  subject: "urn:example:dataset:riverside-air-quality",
  profile: "urn:example:profile:air-quality/1",
  profile_digest: profile_digest,
  content_digest: content_digest,
  now: now,
  bounds: V1.Bounds.maximum()
}

{:ok, assertion_facts} = ContentAssertion.verify_assertion(assertion, expected_assertion)
facts_of.(assertion_facts, [:sub, :gen, :verification, :trust])
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
[
  sub: "urn:example:dataset:riverside-air-quality",
  gen: 1,
  verification: :signature_and_window,
  trust: :not_evaluated
]
```

It fails closed on a changed byte, on edited content (one added space), on the wrong publisher
key and on the wrong audience. The adapter itself refuses an input with a missing field.

```elixir
{:ok, edited_digest} = ContentAssertion.content_digest(dataset <> " ", %{})

[
  tampered_assertion:
    ContentAssertion.verify_assertion(flip_one_byte.(assertion), expected_assertion),
  edited_content:
    ContentAssertion.verify_assertion(assertion, %{
      expected_assertion
      | content_digest: edited_digest
    }),
  wrong_publisher_key:
    ContentAssertion.verify_assertion(assertion, %{
      expected_assertion
      | attestor: %{validity.(publisher) | public_key: holder.public}
    }),
  wrong_audience:
    ContentAssertion.verify_assertion(assertion, %{
      expected_assertion
      | audience: "urn:example:other-archive"
    }),
  missing_field:
    Adapter.sign_content_assertion(Map.delete(assertion_input, :content_digest), publisher_handle)
]
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
[
  tampered_assertion: {:error, :invalid},
  edited_content: {:error, :invalid},
  wrong_publisher_key: {:error, :invalid},
  wrong_audience: {:error, :invalid},
  missing_field: {:error, :invalid_content_assertion}
]
```

## Part 2: ES256 with P-256 keys (contract-major 3)

`BoundedAuthorityReportAdapter.V3` signs the same four standard objects under the
`BAP3-ES256-SHA256` suite: P-256 keys (the 65-byte uncompressed point) and the raw 64-byte
`r || s` signature. Every ECDSA signature has two valid spellings, `s` and `n - s`; the protocol
accepts only the low one. Producing it is the signer's duty, so the adapter normalizes whatever
the handle returns and then checks the result against the public key. The loopback and
content-assertion profiles have no ES256 signer.

First, confirm the demo handle really returns the high spelling.

```elixir
p256_n = 0xFFFFFFFF00000000FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFBCE6FAADA7179E84F3B9CAC2FC632551

low_s? = fn <<_r::binary-size(32), s::unsigned-big-256>> -> s <= div(p256_n, 2) end

signature_of = fn compact ->
  compact |> String.split(".") |> List.last() |> Base.url_decode64!(padding: false)
end

{:ok, device_signature} = Demo.P256Handle.sign("any message", ec_issuer)

[
  device_signature_bytes: byte_size(device_signature),
  device_signature_low_s: low_s?.(device_signature)
]
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
[device_signature_bytes: 64, device_signature_low_s: false]
```

### Issuer and holder under ES256

The protocol package's V3 verify functions take the same input structs as V1
(`V1.Credentials`, `V1.ExpectedRequest`, `V1.TrustedIssuer`, `V1.ExpectedGrant`,
`V1.HistoricalPublicKey` and the expected anchor and key-transition structs). Those structs carry
only the caller's context: trusted keys, the expected issuer, audience and request, the time and
the bounds. The suite is chosen by which module's function you call, and the key bytes inside
the structs are what differ: here a 65-byte P-256 point.

```elixir
ec_issuer_handle = {Demo.P256Handle, ec_issuer}
ec_holder_handle = {Demo.P256Handle, ec_holder}
{:ok, ec_holder_thumbprint} = Demo.P256Handle.thumbprint(ec_holder)

ec_grant_input = %{
  issuer: "https://issuer.example",
  grant_id: "urn:example:grant:inspection-1",
  audiences: ["https://maintenance.example"],
  issued_at: now - 60,
  not_before: now - 60,
  expires_at: now + 3_600,
  holder_thumbprint: ec_holder_thumbprint,
  operations: [%V3.Operation{name: "schedule_inspection", selectors: [:all]}]
}

{:ok, %{grant: ec_grant}} = Adapter.V3.sign_grant(ec_grant_input, ec_issuer_handle)

ec_trusted_issuer = %V1.TrustedIssuer{key_id: ec_issuer.key_id, public_key: ec_issuer.public}

{:ok, ec_grant_facts} =
  V3.verify_grant(ec_grant, ec_trusted_issuer, %{
    expected_grant
    | audience: "https://maintenance.example"
  })

inspection =
  {:object, [{"asset", {:string, "pump-station-2"}}, {"window", {:string, "next-week"}}]}

ec_report = %{
  grant_compact: ec_grant,
  operation: "schedule_inspection",
  method: "POST",
  target_uri: "https://maintenance.example/inspections",
  invocation_id: "3e4f5a6b-7c8d-4e9f-a0b1-c2d3e4f5a6b7",
  cast_arguments: inspection,
  nonce: nil
}

{:ok, %{grant: ^ec_grant, proof: ec_proof}} =
  Adapter.V3.sign_report(ec_report, ec_holder_handle, %{issued_at: now - 5})

ec_credentials = %V1.Credentials{grant: ec_grant, proof: ec_proof}

ec_expected = %V1.ExpectedRequest{
  trusted_issuer: ec_trusted_issuer,
  issuer: "https://issuer.example",
  audience: "https://maintenance.example",
  method: "POST",
  target_uri: "https://maintenance.example/inspections",
  invocation_id: "3e4f5a6b-7c8d-4e9f-a0b1-c2d3e4f5a6b7",
  operation: "schedule_inspection",
  cast_arguments: inspection,
  evaluation_time: now,
  clock_skew: 60,
  proof_max_age: 300,
  nonce: :not_required,
  bounds: V1.Bounds.maximum()
}

{:ok, ec_facts} = V3.check_envelope(ec_credentials, ec_expected)

[
  grant_verified: facts_of.(ec_grant_facts, [:version, :grant_id, :authorization]),
  grant_signature_low_s: low_s?.(signature_of.(ec_grant)),
  envelope_verified: facts_of.(ec_facts, [:version, :operation, :authorization]),
  proof_signature_low_s: low_s?.(signature_of.(ec_proof))
]
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
[
  grant_verified: [
    version: 3,
    grant_id: "urn:example:grant:inspection-1",
    authorization: :not_evaluated
  ],
  grant_signature_low_s: true,
  envelope_verified: [version: 3, operation: "schedule_inspection", authorization: :not_evaluated],
  proof_signature_low_s: true
]
```

The handle returned high-S every time; both compacts carry low-S, and both verify.

### ES256 fails closed, and the majors stay apart

The same checks as Part 1, plus the boundary between suites: a major-1 verifier rejects major-3
credentials, the V3 signer refuses an Ed25519 handle before signing, and it refuses to pair a
major-3 proof with a major-1 grant. `V3.sign_grant/3` has no role-attestation gate (the
`bap-role-attestation/1` profile binds Ed25519 keys only), so it refuses the option rather than
ignore it.

```elixir
ec_stranger_handle = {Demo.P256Handle, p256_key.("stranger-ec-key-1", :holder)}

{:ok, %{proof: ec_stranger_proof}} =
  Adapter.V3.sign_report(ec_report, ec_stranger_handle, %{issued_at: now - 5})

[
  tampered_proof:
    V3.check_envelope(%{ec_credentials | proof: flip_one_byte.(ec_proof)}, ec_expected),
  stranger_holder_key:
    V3.check_envelope(%{ec_credentials | proof: ec_stranger_proof}, ec_expected),
  wrong_audience:
    V3.check_envelope(ec_credentials, %{ec_expected | audience: "https://other.example"}),
  major_1_verifier: V1.check_envelope(ec_credentials, ec_expected),
  ed25519_handle: Adapter.V3.sign_report(ec_report, holder_handle),
  major_1_grant: Adapter.V3.sign_report(%{ec_report | grant_compact: grant}, ec_holder_handle),
  role_attestation_option:
    Adapter.V3.sign_grant(ec_grant_input, ec_issuer_handle, gate.(issuer_attestation, now))
]
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
[
  tampered_proof: {:error, :invalid},
  stranger_holder_key: {:error, :invalid},
  wrong_audience: {:error, :invalid},
  major_1_verifier: {:error, :invalid},
  ed25519_handle: {:error, :invalid_key_handle},
  major_1_grant: {:error, :invalid_report},
  role_attestation_option: {:error, :invalid_role_attestation}
]
```

### Chain checkpoint and key rotation under ES256

```elixir
ec_chain_handle = {Demo.P256Handle, ec_chain_key}
{:ok, ec_chain_fingerprint} = Demo.P256Handle.thumbprint(ec_chain_key)
{:ok, ec_next_chain_fingerprint} = Demo.P256Handle.thumbprint(ec_next_chain_key)

{:ok, %{anchor: ec_anchor}} =
  Adapter.V3.sign_anchor(anchor_input, ec_chain_handle, %{anchored_at: now})

ec_expected_anchor = %{
  expected_anchor
  | key_id: ec_chain_key.key_id,
    key_fingerprint: ec_chain_fingerprint
}

ec_transition_input = %{
  transition_input
  | next_key_id: ec_next_chain_key.key_id,
    next_public_key: ec_next_chain_key.public
}

{:ok, %{key_transition: ec_transition}} =
  Adapter.V3.sign_key_transition(ec_transition_input, ec_chain_handle)

ec_expected_transition = %{
  expected_transition
  | current_key_id: ec_chain_key.key_id,
    current_key_fingerprint: ec_chain_fingerprint,
    next_key_id: ec_next_chain_key.key_id,
    next_key_fingerprint: ec_next_chain_fingerprint
}

{:ok, ec_anchor_facts} =
  V3.verify_historical_anchor(ec_anchor, validity.(ec_chain_key), ec_expected_anchor)

{:ok, ec_transition_facts} =
  V3.verify_key_transition(
    ec_transition,
    validity.(ec_chain_key),
    validity.(ec_next_chain_key),
    ec_expected_transition
  )

[
  anchor_verified: facts_of.(ec_anchor_facts, [:sequence, :verification]),
  anchor_signature_low_s: low_s?.(signature_of.(ec_anchor)),
  tampered_anchor:
    V3.verify_historical_anchor(
      flip_one_byte.(ec_anchor),
      validity.(ec_chain_key),
      ec_expected_anchor
    ),
  transition_verified: facts_of.(ec_transition_facts, [:verification]),
  wrong_successor:
    V3.verify_key_transition(
      ec_transition,
      validity.(ec_chain_key),
      %{validity.(ec_next_chain_key) | public_key: ec_holder.public},
      ec_expected_transition
    ),
  ed25519_successor:
    Adapter.V3.sign_key_transition(
      %{transition_input | next_public_key: next_chain_key.public},
      ec_chain_handle
    )
]
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
[
  anchor_verified: [sequence: 42, verification: :signature_and_window],
  anchor_signature_low_s: true,
  tampered_anchor: {:error, :invalid},
  transition_verified: [verification: :authenticated_transition],
  wrong_successor: {:error, :invalid},
  ed25519_successor: {:error, :invalid_transition}
]
```

## The adapter's own wrong-key guard

Before it returns anything, the adapter checks the signature the handle produced against the
public key the handle reported. A handle that signs with a different key than it reports, or a
P-256 handle that returns OTP's DER encoding instead of the raw `r || s` form, gets
`:signing_failed` and no compact.

```elixir
defmodule Demo.P256DerHandle do
  @moduledoc false
  @behaviour BoundedAuthorityReportAdapter

  # Returns OTP's native DER signature, which is not an ES256 wire form.
  @impl true
  def sign(message, %{private: private}),
    do: {:ok, :crypto.sign(:ecdsa, :sha256, message, [private, :prime256v1])}

  @impl true
  def public_key(key), do: Demo.P256Handle.public_key(key)

  @impl true
  def thumbprint(key), do: Demo.P256Handle.thumbprint(key)
end

stranger = ed25519_key.("stranger-key-2", :holder)
mismatched_holder = {Demo.Ed25519Handle, %{holder | private: stranger.private}}

ec_stranger = p256_key.("stranger-ec-key-2", :holder)
mismatched_ec_holder = {Demo.P256Handle, %{ec_holder | private: ec_stranger.private}}

[
  ed25519_signs_with_another_key: Adapter.sign_report(report, mismatched_holder),
  p256_signs_with_another_key: Adapter.V3.sign_report(ec_report, mismatched_ec_holder),
  p256_returns_der: Adapter.V3.sign_report(ec_report, {Demo.P256DerHandle, ec_holder})
]
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
[
  ed25519_signs_with_another_key: {:error, :signing_failed},
  p256_signs_with_another_key: {:error, :signing_failed},
  p256_returns_der: {:error, :signing_failed}
]
```

## What just happened

| Role         | Who does it in production                            | What ran here                                                                                            |
| ------------ | ---------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| **Issuer**   | the authority runtime, or an issuer with its own key | `sign_grant/3` (with and without a role attestation) and `V3.sign_grant/3` through an issuer-role handle |
| **Holder**   | the edge, using **this adapter**                     | `sign_report/3`, `sign_local_loopback_report/3` and `V3.sign_report/3`: the adapter's one job            |
| **Verifier** | any party with the public protocol package           | `check_envelope/2`, `verify_grant/3` and the other public verify functions                               |

The same handle-based signing path also produced a boundary anchor and a key transition under both
suites, and a content assertion. Every signed object verified with the public protocol package.
Every changed byte, wrong key, wrong audience and wrong context was rejected: by a verifier, with
the protocol's `{:error, :invalid}`, or by the adapter, which then returned no compact, with one
of its closed errors (`:invalid_key_handle`, `:invalid_role_attestation`, `:invalid_report`,
`:invalid_transition`, `:invalid_content_assertion`, `:signing_failed` or
`{:producer_error, :invalid}`).

The adapter signs and nothing more. It is not a verifier, not an issuer runtime, not a transport
and not a database. A real consumer also binds each verified envelope to the authenticated
sender, so a captured envelope cannot be replayed under another identity
(`docs/consumer-integration.md` §8), and records each nonce in a replay ledger (§9). Those steps
belong to the consumer, and that is where a database enters; it is not here.
